WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 117: James Madison and the Secret Debates: Framing the United States Constitution

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.560
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.680
[Rebecca] Today we delve into the intellectual masterpiece of our fourth president, James Madison.

4
00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:20.080
[Rebecca] While Madison was a reluctant wartime leader in 1814, his true genius lay in the quiet halls of political philosophy.

5
00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.920
[Rebecca] In 1787, the American experiment was failing.

6
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:29.680
[Rebecca] The states were squabbling, printing worthless paper money, and teetering toward civil war.

7
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:37.840
[Rebecca] In a sweltering assembly room in Philadelphia, behind nailed shut windows, 55 men debated the future of human liberty.

8
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:45.360
[Rebecca] Through it all, one man guided the debate, captured every word, and crafted the framework that still governs our nation.

9
00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:53.120
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of James Madison and the framing of the Constitution.

10
00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:01.920
[George] In 1786, four years after winning independence from Great Britain, the United States of America was rapidly disintegrating.

11
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:11.280
[George] The nation was governed under the Articles of Confederation, a loose league of friendship designed deliberately with a weak central authority to prevent tyranny.

12
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:24.640
[George] There was no president, no federal judiciary, and no authority for the Continental Congress to collect taxes, regulate interstate commerce, or compel the states to contribute a single dime to the national treasury.

13
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.160
[George] The national debt from the Revolutionary War was crushing.

14
00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:35.040
[George] Paper currency was virtually worthless, and European powers viewed the infant nation with predatory contempt.

15
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:38.800
[George] Individual states behaved like rival sovereign kingdoms.

16
00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:45.440
[George] New York imposed punitive tariffs on firewood brought from Connecticut and cabbages shipped from New Jersey.

17
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:50.000
[George] New Jersey retaliated by taxing New York's lighthouse on Sandy Hook.

18
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:58.880
[George] Rhode Island printed vast sums of depreciated paper money and passed laws forcing merchants to accept it at face value on pain of imprisonment.

19
00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.920
[George] Then, in western Massachusetts, the powder keg exploded.

20
00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:12.760
[George] Hundreds of destitute Revolutionary War veterans, facing debtors' prison and the foreclosure of their family farms, took up arms under Captain Daniel Shays.

21
00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:20.200
[George] Shays' rebellion shut down local courts, attacked federal arsenals, and threatened to plunge the state into civil war.

22
00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.240
[George] Congress was completely powerless to intervene.

23
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:29.720
[George] Across the Atlantic, European monarchies gleefully predicted that the American experiment was dead in the water.

24
00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:42.040
[George] To thoughtful patriots like James Madison, it was painfully clear, unless the national government was fundamentally rebuilt, the hard-won fruits of the revolution would be lost to anarchy and despotism.

25
00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:54.040
[George] In May 1787, delegates from across the States arrived at the Pennsylvania Statehouse in Philadelphia, the very room where the Declaration of Independence had been signed eleven years earlier.

26
00:02:54.040 --> 00:03:01.640
[George] Officially, their mandate from Congress was strictly limited to propose amendments to the existing Articles of Confederation.

27
00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:07.400
[George] But James Madison, representing Virginia, had no intention of patching a sinking ship.

28
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:14.120
[George] Arriving eleven days before a quorum was achieved, Madison had spent years preparing for this singular moment.

29
00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:20.520
[George] From his estate at Montpelier, Madison had engaged in an exhaustive study of human political systems.

30
00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:37.640
[George] He had requested his close friend Thomas Jefferson, then serving as minister in Paris, to send him trunks packed with every major philosophical and historical work on Confederacies, from the Lycian and Amphictionic Leagues of Ancient Greece to the Swiss cantons and the Dutch Republic.

31
00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:44.920
[George] Madison devoured these volumes, meticulously tabulating the structural defects that caused every past republic to collapse.

32
00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:53.920
[George] His conclusion was definitive: a confederation of sovereign states could never maintain internal stability or protect individual liberty.

33
00:03:53.920 --> 00:04:01.600
[George] What America needed was not a confederation, but a sovereign national republic deriving its authority directly from the people.

34
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:08.560
[George] On May 25, the convention officially commenced, electing George Washington as its presiding officer.

35
00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:14.720
[George] Washington's solemn, commanding presence lent immediate gravitas and legitimacy to the gathering.

36
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:17.840
[George] The delegates' very first decision was radical.

37
00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:23.040
[George] They voted to impose absolute, impenetrable secrecy upon all their proceedings.

38
00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:32.000
[George] The tall windows of the assembly room were nailed shut and heavy curtains drawn, despite the oppressive, sweltering heat of a Philadelphia summer.

39
00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:44.880
[George] Guards were posted at the doors, and delegates pledged on their honor not to reveal a word of the debates to the press or public, ensuring they could speak freely, change their minds, and debate without public hysteria.

40
00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:55.200
[George] On May 29, Virginia Governor Edmund Randolph rose to introduce fifteen resolutions prepared by James Madison, known to history as the Virginia Plan.

41
00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:00.560
[George] It was an intellectual bombshell, rather than revising the Articles of Confederation.

42
00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:16.160
[George] The Virginia Plan proposed throwing them in the wastebasket and creating a completely new national government consisting of three distinct, co-equal branches: a bicameral legislature, an independent national executive, and a federal judiciary.

43
00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:26.160
[George] Crucially, Madison proposed that representation in both houses of Congress should be proportional, based on the population or financial contributions of each state.

44
00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:36.840
[George] Under the Articles, every state had possessed an equal vote, meaning that tiny Delaware or Rhode Island held equal power to massive, populous Virginia or Pennsylvania.

45
00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:44.120
[George] Madison argued passionately that a government of the people must represent people, not imaginary sovereign borders.

46
00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:54.680
[George] Furthermore, Madison proposed giving Congress the power to veto any state law that conflicted with the national constitution, establishing federal supremacy once and for all.

47
00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.400
[George] The smaller states erupted in fury.

48
00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:05.160
[George] Delegates from New Jersey, Delaware, Connecticut, and Maryland saw proportional representation as an existential threat.

49
00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:13.640
[George] William Patterson of New Jersey countered with the New Jersey plan, which proposed retaining the single house legislature with equal state voting.

50
00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.160
[George] For weeks, tempers flared in the suffocating heat.

51
00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:32.520
[George] Patterson warned that small states would never submit to the tyranny of the large states, and Gunning Bedford of Delaware went so far as to threaten that if the small states were marginalized, they would seek foreign alliances with European monarchs to protect their independence.

52
00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:36.280
[George] The convention was deadlocked on the verge of complete collapse.

53
00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:47.560
[George] In early July, with the convention on the brink of dissolution, eighty-one-year-old Benjamin Franklin, the venerable sage of Philadelphia, called for calm and divine guidance.

54
00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:56.280
[George] He noted that when a broad table is to be made, and the boards do not fit, the carpenter planes a little from both sides until the joint is true.

55
00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:03.080
[George] Out of this crisis emerged the Great Compromise, spearheaded by Roger Sherman and Oliver Ellsworth of Connecticut.

56
00:07:03.080 --> 00:07:09.880
[George] The Connecticut Compromise proposed a dual legislature that resolved the deadlock between large and small states.

57
00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:23.040
[George] In the lower house, the House of Representatives, representation would be determined strictly by population, satisfying the large states and ensuring that revenue bills originated directly from the representatives of the people.

58
00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:35.280
[George] In the upper house, the Senate, every state would receive equal representation, two senators each, chosen initially by state legislatures, protecting the sovereignty of smaller states.

59
00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:45.360
[George] Madison privately resisted this compromise, believing that equal representation in the Senate was a defect that violated the core democratic principle of majority rule.

60
00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.640
[George] Yet Madison was also a supreme pragmatist.

61
00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:53.360
[George] He recognized that without this compromise, there would be no union at all.

62
00:07:53.360 --> 00:08:01.520
[George] He acquiesced, and turned his formidable intellect toward the remaining monumental challenges, defining the powers of the presidency.

63
00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:16.240
[George] Establishing an electoral college to balance popular will and state authority, creating an independent Supreme Court, and crafting an amendment process that would allow the Constitution to adapt to future generations without resorting to armed revolution.

64
00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:27.680
[George] While fifty-four other men argued, shouted, walked out, or cooled themselves with fans in the stifling chamber, James Madison performed an act of heroic historical preservation.

65
00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:36.240
[George] Choosing a seat at the center table directly in front of George Washington's dais, Madison appointed himself the convention's unofficial scribe.

66
00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:44.640
[George] For four months, during six days a week and up to six hours a day, Madison sat with quill and paper, recording the proceedings.

67
00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:54.160
[George] He wrote that he did not lose a single day, nor more than a fraction of an hour from any sitting, nor did he allow any significant speech to go unrecorded.

68
00:08:54.160 --> 00:09:03.240
[George] He devised his own personal system of shorthand to capture the exact phrasing, philosophical arguments, and fiery rhetoric of every speaker.

69
00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:17.640
[George] Every night, while other delegates gathered at taverns to drink and socialize, Madison sat by candlelight in his rented boarding room, laboriously deciphering his notes and writing out full verbatim transcripts before his memory faded.

70
00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:25.320
[George] Madison was so devoted to the principle of convention secrecy that he refused to allow his notes to be published during his lifetime.

71
00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:34.520
[George] He believed that the Constitution should stand on the objective meaning of its ratified text, rather than the private motives or heated arguments of its framers.

72
00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:40.440
[George] Madison lived to be 85 years old, the very last surviving signer of the Constitution.

73
00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:52.040
[George] Upon his death in 1836, his notes were purchased by Congress and published, providing humanity with its most intimate, profound record of how the American Republic was conceived.

74
00:09:52.040 --> 00:10:00.200
[George] On September 17, 1787, 39 delegates signed the completed Constitution of the United States.

75
00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:12.520
[George] As Benjamin Franklin looked toward the sun painted on the back of George Washington's chair, he observed that throughout the four months of doubt and struggle, he had often wondered whether it was a rising or a setting sun.

76
00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:19.000
[George] Now at length, Franklin declared, I have the happiness to know that it is a rising and not a setting sun.

77
00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:22.040
[George] Yet signing the parchment was only half the battle.

78
00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:29.560
[George] Under Article 7, the Constitution had to be ratified by special conventions in at least nine of the thirteen states.

79
00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] Fierce opposition erupted from the antifederalists, including luminaries like Patrick Henry and George Mason, who argued that the Constitution created an imperial government that would crush local self-governance and lacked a bill of rights to protect personal freedoms.

80
00:10:45.040 --> 00:10:56.880
[George] To defend the Constitution, Madison joined forces with Alexander Hamilton and John Jay in New York to write a series of 85 brilliant newspaper essays under the pseudonym Publius.

81
00:10:56.880 --> 00:11:04.720
[George] These essays, known collectively as the Federalist Papers, remained the supreme masterpiece of American political thought.

82
00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:09.520
[George] Madison authored 29 of the essays, including the Immortal Federalist No.

83
00:11:09.520 --> 00:11:15.440
[George] 10, in which he refuted the ancient belief that republics could only survive in small territories.

84
00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:28.160
[George] Madison argued that an extended republic was actually more stable, because a multiplicity of competing factions, interests, and religious sects would prevent any single tyrannical majority from oppressing the rest.

85
00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:29.520
[George] In Federalist No.

86
00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:34.480
[George] 51, Madison penned the definitive justification for checks and balances.

87
00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:38.160
[George] If men were angels, no government would be necessary.

88
00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:41.120
[George] Ambition must be made to counteract ambition.

89
00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:47.760
[George] In June 1788, the Virginia Ratifying Convention met in Richmond for a Titanic showdown.

90
00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:58.000
[George] Patrick Henry, the fiery orator of Give Me Liberty or Give Me Death, delivered thunderous speeches warning that the Constitution was a gilded trap leading to monarchy.

91
00:11:58.000 --> 00:12:11.920
[George] James Madison, frail, quiet, and speaking so softly that the stenographers struggled to hear him, systematically dismantled Henry's arguments with relentless logic, constitutional mastery, and undeniable fact.

92
00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:20.000
[George] Madison pledged that as soon as the new government took office, he would introduce a federal bill of rights to guarantee fundamental liberties.

93
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:25.440
[George] Virginia voted to ratify, and the Constitution became the supreme law of the land.

94
00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.440
[George] Elected to the first Congress, Madison fulfilled his promise.

95
00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:50.120
[George] Sorting through hundreds of proposed amendments, he drafted the twelve amendments that Congress approved, ten of which were ratified by the states in 1791 as the Bill of Rights, guaranteeing freedom of speech, freedom of the press, religious liberty, trial by jury, and protection against unreasonable searches and seizures.

96
00:12:50.120 --> 00:12:57.000
[George] James Madison's genius was not that he created a perfect government, but that he created a self-correcting one.

97
00:12:57.000 --> 00:13:13.080
[George] By designing a system of divided powers, co-equal checks, and constitutional amendments, he built a durable framework capable of enduring civil war, global depression, technological transformation, and two and a half centuries of tumultuous change.

98
00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:21.000
[George] He proved to the world that a free people could govern themselves through reflection and choice, rather than accident and force.

99
00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:27.640
[Rebecca] James Madison gave the American Republic its intellectual spine and its enduring framework of liberty.

100
00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:37.400
[Rebecca] From the sweltering heat of the Philadelphia Convention to the foundational words of the Bill of Rights, his quiet brilliance continues to protect the rights of every American citizen.

101
00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:49.960
[Rebecca] Next week, we turn to our fifth president, James Monroe, and the bold declaration in 1823 that warned the empires of Europe to keep their hands off the Western Hemisphere forever.

102
00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:54.120
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

